This is Jesus's Instructions for Redtell Via Macbook

Run RedTell + modifications
Before anything, inside the RedTell folder, make a new folder called “Data”
•	Segmentation
      o	Make sure to label your pictures correctly (nanoplastic used, concentration, time of exposure, etc.) so you know which is which.
      o	Place your images in a folder (directory) called “original_pictures”. This folder will be inside “Data”
      o	Make a new folder called “images”, also inside “Data”
      o	Run crop_pictures.py or crop_colored_pictures.py
      o	A folder with pictures of 563 * 563 pixels will be created from the original pictures (cut in 4 equal parts) and they will be saved on “images”
      o	If using a Mac, run the segmentation code using: KMP_DUPLICATE_LIB_OK=TRUE python redtell.py --funct segmentation --data Data
      o	You will get two new folders called “masks” and “segmentation_results”
      o	Check that those two folders have actual pictures with masks and with overlayed masks on the images.
      o	Success!!
•	Features extraction
      o	Run remove_edge_cells.py using: python remove_edge_cells.py – Data “data”
      o	This script will remove cells that are in the edge of the pictures, so the features are subtracted only from whole cells. It will also resize the masks.
      o	Run features_extraction by using: KMP_DUPLICATE_LIB_OK=TRUE python redtell.py --funct  extract_features --data Data --channel mask bf
      o	You will get a .csv file called “features”
      o	Check that the file actually contains information.
      o	After checking features.csv, you will run custom_features.py. Make sure to change the data folder before running the script. This script will add features that are not originally part of RedTell but that are useful for dead cell identification. After running the script, it will add extra columns to features.csv, make sure you see them when you open the file.
      o	Success!!
•	Annotations
      o	Run KMP_DUPLICATE_LIB_OK=TRUE python redtell.py --funct annotate --data Data –num_cells “at least 200”
      o	A folder called “annotations” will be created and pictures of individual cells will be saved.
      o	A .csv file called annotations will be created where each cell will be assigned a number (ex: 1 through 200) and that will correspond to the name of the pictures saved on the annotations folder.
      o	You will assign a label to cells: healthy, stressed, crenated, dead. You will need to have the same number of labeled cells for each class (or at least similar numbers). 
      o	After finishing your annotations, you will run concatenate_annotations_features.py, again, make sure you’re using the correct data folder. This script will add your annotations to the corresponding cell on the features.csv file.
      o	Open features.csv and make sure you see the column called “label”
      o	Lastly, you will run python clean_features_by_size.py to remove object that were predicted to be cells but are actually small or big debris on the pictures.
      o	Success!!
•	Classification
      o	For classification, there are two options, the original RedTell classification script, and my own classification script. I will give you instructions for both, and you can choose which one you want to use.
      o	Jesus’s classifier:
            	You will run python hierarchical_classifier.py (don’t forget to change the data folder name).
            	This script will do two classifications, first it will do a binary classification where it will predict dead and healthy cells, and then it will do a multiclass classification for morphology. At the end it will create confusion matrices for each classification stage and the final combined confusion matrix, and it will create files with the training diagnostics where you can see your annotated cells and their predicted label, as well as the predicted labels for all non-annotated cells.
            	Success!!
      o	RedTell classifier:
            	Since the creator of RedTell didn’t finish the classification script, you need to run the classifier directly from the main.py script inside the classification folder, you can copy this to your terminal to run it: 
            PYTHONPATH=classification/src python classification/src/main.py \
              -f “your_data_folder”/features.csv \
              -o “your_data_folder”/classification_results \
              --label label \
              --cell cell_id \
              --group image \
              -p random
•	Analysis
      o	Run python morphology_analysis.py, change the directory where your features.csv file is in on the script and run it, it will separate the data by groups, and it will give you the information needed for the graphs of dead cells.

